# Audio Transcription Project

This repository is the starting point for a PyTorch-based automatic music transcription system: audio in, symbolic note output out.

The target is not speech-to-text. The target is music-to-notes, with a practical MVP focused on piano-first transcription.

## MVP technical direction

The first model is intentionally narrow and buildable:

- input: waveform audio
- features: log-mel spectrograms from `torchaudio`
- supervision: aligned MIDI files
- outputs: onset probabilities and frame activity probabilities for the 88 piano keys
- decoder: note-event extraction from onset/frame predictions
- symbolic result: note events now, MIDI export next, MusicXML later

Why this baseline:

- it matches the structure of the problem better than text-token decoding
- it is a standard and defensible AMT baseline
- it can be trained on datasets such as MAESTRO
- it gives a clean path to later upgrades such as Onsets and Frames variants, Conformers, or diffusion-assisted symbolic cleanup

## Project layout

```text
docs/                    architecture and implementation notes
src/audio_project/       application package
src/audio_project/data/  dataset, MIDI parsing, audio transforms
src/audio_project/models/model definitions
src/audio_project/training/training loop and config wiring
src/audio_project/inference/ offline transcription and note decoding
```

## Suggested build phases

1. Baseline MVP
   - train an onset/frame model on aligned audio + MIDI
   - decode note events locally
   - evaluate note precision, recall, and F1
2. Quality improvements
   - stronger encoder such as a Conformer or U-Net style temporal stack
   - velocity estimation and sustain pedal handling
   - better note decoding and score cleanup
3. Integration handoff
   - package model loading and note decoding cleanly for the backend team
   - define artifact format and preprocessing contract
   - document the symbolic output format for the website team

## Example training dataset format

Use a JSONL manifest with one sample per line:

```json
{"audio_path": "data/audio/performance_001.wav", "midi_path": "data/midi/performance_001.mid"}
{"audio_path": "data/audio/performance_002.wav", "midi_path": "data/midi/performance_002.mid"}
```

## Getting started

1. Create a virtual environment.
2. Install the package in editable mode with the dependencies from `pyproject.toml`.
3. Prepare `train.jsonl`, `valid.jsonl`, and `test.jsonl` manifests from aligned audio and MIDI.
4. Run the training entrypoint.
5. Decode note events from a checkpoint and hand the artifact contract to the backend team.

Each training run now writes:

- checkpoints
- `history.json`
- `history.csv`
- `training_summary.png` with loss, onset F1, frame F1, and frame accuracy curves

## Useful commands

Train on the larger synthetic dataset:

```bash
MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/matplotlib PYTHONPATH=src python -m audio_project.training.train \
  --device cpu \
  --epochs 6 \
  --batch-size 8 \
  --learning-rate 0.001 \
  --min-learning-rate 0.0001 \
  --train-manifest data/synthetic_piano_large/manifests/train.jsonl \
  --valid-manifest data/synthetic_piano_large/manifests/valid.jsonl \
  --checkpoint-dir artifacts/synthetic_run_eval_ready
```

Transcribe an audio file to JSON and MIDI:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src python scripts/transcribe_audio.py \
  --audio data/synthetic_piano_large/valid/audio/valid_0000.wav \
  --checkpoint artifacts/synthetic_run_eval_ready/music_transcription_epoch_006.pt \
  --onset-threshold 0.2 \
  --frame-threshold 0.35 \
  --min-note-duration 0.04 \
  --json-out artifacts/sample_transcription.json \
  --midi-out artifacts/sample_transcription.mid
```

Evaluate a checkpoint with note-level metrics and diagnostic images:

```bash
MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/matplotlib PYTHONPATH=src python scripts/evaluate_checkpoint.py \
  --checkpoint artifacts/synthetic_run_eval_ready/music_transcription_epoch_006.pt \
  --manifest data/synthetic_piano_large/manifests/valid.jsonl \
  --output-dir artifacts/synthetic_run_eval_ready/note_eval \
  --sample-limit 16
```

## Immediate next steps

- decide whether the MVP is piano-only or broader instrumentation
- start with a piano dataset such as MAESTRO rather than arbitrary web scraping
- train the baseline model and measure note-level F1
- align checkpoint loading and symbolic output format with the backend team

